me` match, so `/opt/homebrew/bin/` gets consumed by `\\S*` while the actual basename gets captured correctly. I'm going to write a straightforward regex pattern using Go's regexp package to handle this extraction cleanly. I'm testing different regex patterns to match basename with various path prefixes. The character class approach `[\\w/.-]*g?basename` correctly handles the test cases—it matches the full path for `/opt/homebrew/bin/basename`, just `gbasename` when there's a leading quote, and `basename` on its own. This should work, though I'm wondering if there's a simpler approach.\n\nActually, I can just use two straightforward replacements: one to handle `gbasename` directly, and a regex to strip the full path from quoted strings like `'/opt/homebrew/bin/basename'` down to just `'basename'`. Looking at the actual reference output, the full path appears without the `g` prefix, so the first replacement might be redundant for this specific case, but it's worth keeping for robustness.\n\nRefining the approach with a broader regex pattern... I'm working through how the regex handles the full path case—when it encounters `/opt/homebrew/bin/basename`, the optional group matches nothing at the quote, then the pattern backtracks and successfully matches the slash-delimited path, replacing it with just `basename`. This approach handles both the error message and the full path correctly.\n\nNow I need to add the `regexp` import and update the test file to verify this works.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1135,"cache_read_input_tokens":38657,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1135},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42687951-365f-454b-a978-a292460ae6b8","uuid":"d6dc4146-a057-4d91-8960-87085e986d28"}
